Layer 2: process lifecycle

Process lifecycle starts real binaries inside the namespaces that
Layer 1 built, and restarts them with precise meanings. The mesh
moves packets; this layer moves processes.

Starting a node

spawn_one_net (sim/sim.c:1256) does the work for one node. In
plain words, it prepares an isolated home for the node and then
forks:

1. Make a per-node config home (STATEDIR/nodei).
2. Copy the shared CA certificates into it, so the node can prove
   who it is.
3. Copy the shared gossip key into it, so the node joins this fleet
   and no other.
4. Write the node's config: a fresh id, its memberlist and advertise
   addresses on the underlay.
5. Fork. In the child, enter the node's network namespace (except
   node 0 on the frozen check/ path, which lives in the host
   namespace; the isolated _all path enters node 0 into its own
   namespace), point config home at
   the prepared directory, redirect output into a log pipe, and exec
   the binary.
6. In the parent, place the child in its per-node cgroup (so each
   node's containers get their own subtree) and keep the pid and the
   log pipe.

Variants on top: sim_spawn_concord_net (plain),
sim_spawn_concord_skew_net (one node with CONCORD_CLOCK_OFFSET
set, a test time hook the binary provides),
sim_spawn_concord_anchor_net (one node started with --anchor and
every config pointed at it), and sim_spawn_concord_debug_net
(verbose logging for diagnosis only). All four are declared in
sim/sim.h:157-195. New code uses these _net forms with an
explicit SimNet. The legacy wrappers without _net
(sim/sim.h:201-249) stay frozen for check/.

Note that seeding shells out several times per node (mkdir, cp,
config printf). That cost repeats for every node of every check/
scenario and is one of the known speedups in /docs/speed.

Restarting a node: five meanings

"Restart" sounds like one thing but the code pins five different
meanings. Each meaning tests a different durability promise, so
driver/concord.h:7-20 keeps them as five enum values
(CONCORD_FRESH, CONCORD_PRESERVE, CONCORD_REKEY_ROTATE,
CONCORD_KEY_WIPE, CONCORD_WIPE_STATE). The check/ files below
are the frozen origin shapes. New code calls concord_restart
(driver/concord.h:68) with one of the five.

1. Fresh identity, wiped directory. CONCORD_FRESH:
   sim_restart_concord_net deletes the node's whole home and
   starts it as a stranger. Used by check/concord_restart.c:95-102,
   and while partitioned or mid-sync by check/concord_killisolated.c,
   check/concord_killmidsync.c, check/concord_scale_churn.c, and
   check/concord_spam.c (check/-only callers).
2. Same directory preserved. CONCORD_PRESERVE:
   restart_preserve in check/concord_cursor_resume.c:152-198:
   SIGKILL the process but keep identity, journal, cursor store, and
   pins. Only the process dies. Proves cursors resume instead of
   re-pulling everything.
3. Same directory, rotated key. CONCORD_REKEY_ROTATE:
   restart_rotated in check/concord_rotate.c:136-189 SIGTERMs the
   node, runs node rotate-key for a generation bump, and reforks in
   place. TERM, not KILL, so the daemon flushes before exit
   (driver/concord.h:66-67). Proves rotation behavior.
4. Same directory, wiped key. CONCORD_KEY_WIPE:
   restart_node in check/concord_pinalarm.c:159-229 with the wipe
   half: SIGKILL the node and delete only the Noise secret key, so
   reboot mints a fresh key at the same generation. Proves
   first-key-pins behavior. This is distinct from rotate: wipe keeps
   the generation, rotate bumps it.
5. Same identity, wiped state. CONCORD_WIPE_STATE:
   restart_wiped in check/concord_reinstall.c:153-215: keep the id
   and key, delete journal plus kv store, rejoin empty. Proves peers
   fall back past stale cursors and the node re-pulls full history.

Two more cases kill and never restart: the elected leader in
check/concord_failover.c:298-305 (a successor must take over
scheduling) and the workload owner in check/concord_orphan.c:374-376
(a survivor must reassign its work at a bumped epoch). Both are
check/-only. New code calls concord_kill (driver/concord.h:56)
for the same stay-dead meaning.

What the Weld driver provides

driver/weld.h provides the same verbs with different bodies.
weld_spawn_node (driver/weld.h:99) starts a weld echo node per
netns with a domain id. weld_pub (driver/weld.h:133) publishes
once from a node netns. weld_ros_peer_start
(driver/weld.h:149) starts a ROS peer beside a node where the
scenario needs one. Kill and restart come with stated durability:
WeldDurability (driver/weld.h:60-65) names WELD_FRESH (wiped
home) and WELD_PRESERVE (same home), matching the way Concord
nodes keep their journal. ROS peers are usually stateless and
restart fresh. The mesh, the faults, and the runner never learn
these details. They call spawn, kill, and restart with a durability
argument and read back what the driver reports.

A hook that exists but sleeps

sim_spawn_concord_skew_net sets CONCORD_CLOCK_OFFSET for one node,
shifting that node's clock. No check/ scenario uses it today. It is the
only SUT-clock control the harness has, and /docs/speed explains
why touching SUT clocks is delicate. SUT here means the system under
test: the binary the harness measures, Concord or Weld.
